Adductor Strain (Groin Strain)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Acute medial groin pain, Tenderness over pubic ramus, Pain on resisted adduction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-003
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe groin pain following sudden trauma causing immediate functional impairment and inability to ambulate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage determined the patient suffered an acute traumatic injury resulting in severe medial thigh pain, significant swelling, and inability to bear weight. Urgent physical assessment was critical to rule out high-grade musculotendinous rupture or avulsion fracture, and to implement safe acute pain management and immobilization protocols to prevent further tissue disruption prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I stepped wrong and felt a massive pop in my inner thigh; the pain is blinding, and I can't walk."
History of Present Illness: Acute onset of sharp pain in the right groin and medial thigh following a sudden change of direction. Timeline: Immediate sensation of a pop/snap at the precise moment of injury; rapid onset of localized swelling in the medial upper thigh within the last 4 hours; constant throbbing ache at rest, converting to severe, sharp pain upon any minor leg movement. Patient crawled to the living room and remains stranded.
Functional Status: Acutely incapacitated, unable to walk or safely mobilize.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; completely unable to access basic necessities (food/water/bathroom) due to sudden immobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, explicitly guarding the right lower limb.
Physical Exam: Swelling localized to the medial upper right thigh. Focal bruising beginning to appear. Palpable tenderness along the adductor muscle bellies and explicitly at their origin on the inferior pubic ramus. Pain is severely exacerbated by active adduction of the hip against resistance and provoked by passive forceful abduction. No palpable gross defect in the muscle belly. Hip joint range of motion normal but limited by adductor guarding.
Environmental Observations: Patient situated on the floor; physical assistance required to transfer to a stable resting surface.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Adductor Strain (Grade II) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Pubic Ramus Avulsion Fracture - Ruled out clinically due to lack of distinct bony crepitus and localized bony point tenderness compared to the musculotendinous junction, though requires formal imaging.

Inguinal Hernia (Strangulated) - Ruled out due to absence of a palpable mass, no bowel symptoms, and mechanism directly linked to musculoskeletal exertion.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out due to acute traumatic mechanism of injury, isolated medial swelling rather than diffuse calf/leg swelling, and normal distal pulses.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained an acute traumatic injury to the medial thigh, presenting with classic signs of an adductor musculotendinous strain, including an audible pop, focal tenderness, and significant pain on resisted adduction. The resulting functional deficit left the patient stranded on the floor, living alone, and unable to perform essential activities of daily living. Postponing medical evaluation until the next in-hours period would have resulted in unacceptable prolonged suffering, dehydration risk, and potential further structural damage from unassisted, improper mobilization attempts. Urgent after-hours assessment safely diagnosed the soft tissue injury, ruled out surgical vascular or intra-abdominal pathology, and facilitated assisted transfer and the initiation of pharmacological and mechanical management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Paracetamol 1g QID and Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Keep the legs closely approximated using a pillow between the knees while resting. Consequence: Prevention of sudden painful stretching of the torn muscle, leading to uninterrupted rest (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply a compression bandage to the upper thigh. Consequence: Reduction in swelling and feeling of tissue support, granting confidence in minor movements (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Arranged standard crutches for partial weight-bearing mobilization.
Follow-up: Routine GP review and pelvic/thigh ultrasound in 48-72 hours to confirm injury grading.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden massive swelling of the entire leg, numbness in the leg, or if the foot becomes cold and pale.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal ultrasound imaging to evaluate the exact grade of the tear. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Acute pain and swelling will peak over 48 hours. Bruising will migrate down the leg. Healing requires gradual, guided rehabilitation over 4-8 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if your entire leg swells rapidly, if you lose feeling in your foot, or if you develop severe, unmanageable pain despite medications.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The dual analgesia targets both the pain signals in the brain and the local inflammatory swelling at the tear site. Crutches prevent the muscle from bearing the load of your body weight while the initial scar tissue forms.
Actionable Steps: Use the pillow between your knees in bed; this prevents accidental painful stretching and allows you to sleep through the night.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Adductor (groin) muscle strain. Rest, compress, use crutches, and take prescribed painkillers. Book a GP appointment in 2-3 days for an ultrasound.

SMS EDUCATION

